  "lineage": "Garifuna (Black-Carib) Honduran-Belizean-Guatemalan-Nicaraguan Caribbean-coast Afro-Amerindian song-and-dance tradition descended from the 1797 British-deportation of the surviving Carib-Arawak-Afro-Caribbean people from St-Vincent to Roatan-island (now eastern-Caribbean-coast Central-America). The canonical punta ensemble: three garaon barrel-drums (primero high-improvisation, segunda middle-rhythm, tercera bass-drum) with maracas (sisira), conch-shell-trumpet, and call-and-response female-vocal-leadership. The 2/4 rapid-tempo punta-rhythm accentuates the dance characterized by virtuosic hip-and-glute movement. (under the broader Garifuna language-music-and-dance category). The 1980s-onward punta-rock electrified-and-commercialized variant brought guitar-and-keyboard to the genre.",
